## In-situ self-assembled hollow urchins F-Co-MOF on rGO as advanced anodes for
## lithium-ion and sodium-ion batteries
Ruipeng Wei,\(^{a}\) Yutao Dong,\(^{b,\ast}\) Yingying Zhang,\(^{a}\) Ran Zhang,\(^{a}\) Mohammed A. Al-Tahan,\(^{a}\) and Jianmin Zhang,\(^{a,\ast}\)
\(^{a}\)Center of Green Catalysis, and College of Chemistry, Zhengzhou University, Zhengzhou 450001, China
\(^{b}\)Analyses and Testing Center, Zhengzhou University of Technology, Zhengzhou 450044, China
\(\ast\)Corresponding authors:
E-mail: dongok0306@163.com (Y. Dong), zhjm@zzu.edu.cn (J. Zhang)
Abstract
To obtain MOFs materials with good electrochemical performance in both lithium-ion batteries (LIBs) and sodium-ion batteries (SIBs), a kind of hollow urchins Co-MOF with doping fluorine (F) was in-situ assembled on reduced graphene oxide (rGO) using a simple solvothermal reaction. According to XRD, XPS and EDS mapping analysis, the molecular structure should be \(\mathrm{Co_{2}[F_{x}(OH)_{1-x}]_{2}(C_{8}O_{4}H_{4})}\) (denoted as F-Co-MOF). When the composite material is used as active material to assemble LIBs, it not only presents the outstanding reversible capacity (1202.0 mA h g\(^{-1}\) at 0.1 A g\(^{-1}\)), but also gives the excellent rate performance and cycle performance (771.5 mA h g\(^{-1}\) at 2 A g\(^{-1}\) after 550 repeated cycles). The remarkable lithium storage capacity of F-Co-MOF/rGO is also reflected in the full cell, where it can still maintain a high capacity of 165.2 mA h g\(^{-1}\) after 300 cycles at 0.2 A g\(^{-1}\). It benefits from the synergistic effect of F-Co-MOF and high conductive rGO networks, so that the reversibility of lithium and sodium storage can be improved. This kind of F doped solvothermal synthesis of MOFs is of great significance for the exploration of high performance materials.
Keywords: Metal organic frameworks, Fluorine doping, Reduced graphene oxide, Hollow urchins, Lithium and sodium storage
## 1. Introduction
Along with the rapid growth of population and the continuous deterioration of environment, people's demand for new energy is more urgent than ever before. The rise of new energy sources such as geothermal energy, nuclear fusion energy, biomass energy has slowed down the consumption of fossil energy, which is crucial in environmental
protection and avoiding energy depletion. However, due to high costs or improper geographic location, the use of these new energy sources may be restricted. Consequently, it is worth exploring how to store and utilize energy more reasonably and more fully. LIBs and SIBs, as a new generation of energy storage devices, have great potential in energy storage and utilization due to their high energy density and cycle stability [1-9]. Nowadays, although LIBs have developed rapidly in the extensive use of electronic products and electric vehicles, people still have higher requirements for their performance [10-15]. For this reason, it is urgent to develop more excellent electrode materials.
The metal organic frameworks (MOFs) have received extensive attention and research due to its diversity of structural and composition, large specific surface area and controllability of morphology [16-23]. However, it has been found from previous studies that MOFs are mostly used as precursors for the synthesis of various electrode materials in the field of energy storage. For example, using MOFs as precursors to synthesize other electrode materials (such as selenides, carbon, phosphides, transition metal oxides and sulfides) has become a very effective general method [24-32]. Nevertheless, the synthesis process of MOFs derivative often causes certain damage to the original morphology, which in turn affects its electrochemical performance [33, 34]. So, if MOFs themselves can be directly used as active materials for LIBs and SIBs, this problem can be avoided. At the same time, more and more studies have discovered the great potential of MOFs as electrode materials, because their chemical properties and special architecture are expected to accommodate the insertion/extraction of \(\mathrm{Li^{+}}\) ions [35]. Some studies have reported that MOFs can be used as electrode material directly, but due to the low inherent
conductivity of MOFs, the ideal electrochemical performance has not been achieved [36-39]. X. M. Lin et al. reported the lithium storage performance of Cd(HTTPCA) as an active material for LIBs, and gave a reversible capacity of \(302\,\mathrm{mA\,h\,g^{-1}}\) after cycling 100 cycles at \(0.1\,\mathrm{A\,g^{-1}}\) [39]. Therefore, it is still worth exploring how MOF materials can be better developed as electrode materials for LIBs and SIBs and achieve excellent performance.
In order to make the prepared materials have a more dazzling performance in the field of energy storage, reducing the size, optimizing the morphology and compounding with the conductive material are widely recognized and extremely effective methods [40-46]. These simple and effective strategies can also be referred to in the preparation of MOFs with high electrochemical performance. In this work, a simple and effective strategy was presented to synthesize Co-MOF with doping F, which was in-situ assembled on reduced graphene oxide (rGO). It is exceedingly critical to choose a suitable metal ion as the central ion of MOFs. The reason why we chose cobalt ion \(\left(\mathrm{Co}^{2+}\right)\) is that it has been reported that working electrode materials containing transition metals usually exhibit unusual electrochemical performance [47]. The choice of organic ligand PTA is because H. Hu et al. has reported that PTA may have 8 active sites for \(\mathrm{Li^{+}}\) ions insertion, so that the material containing PTA will have a higher theoretical capacity [48]. The doping of F has two main functions. First, F can increase the crystallinity of microporous materials. That is it beneficial to the formation of high crystalline phases in MOF, for example, D.-Y. Hong et al. found that the use of F during the synthesis of MIL-101(Cr) promoted the effective nucleation of MIL-101(Cr) [49]. Secondly, it has been reported
that F doping can enhance lithiation/delithiation reversibility and lithium storage performance in literatures [50, 51]. When synthesizing the bimetallic oxide \(\mathrm{MnWO_{4}}\), W. Wang et al. obtained through comparison that the doping of F is a key factor to improve the insertion capacity and promote stability. Circulating for 150 cycles at \(0.2\mathrm{~A~g^{-1}}\), the capacity retention rate of F-doped \(\mathrm{MnWO_{4}}\) increased by \(62.5\%\) compared to the undoped [50]. In the preparation of functional electrode materials, the problem of low conductivity is usually solved by compounding with materials with good conductivity. Considering the organic materials may decompose at high temperatures, rGO is selected to enhance conductivity. As we expected, after a series of characterizations and tests, the F-Co-MOF/rGO composite with hollow urchin nanostructures showed satisfactory electrochemical performance. This simple and effective method opens up a possible way for the preparation of other MOFs with excellent electrochemical properties.
## 2. Experimental sections
2.1. Synthesis of F-Co-MOF/rGO.
Synthesis of the F-Co-MOF/rGO was adapted from a modified procedure reported in literature [52]. Typically, 1 mmol CTAB (Hexadecyltrimethylammonium bromide, 0.3645 g) was added as a surfactant into \(50\;\mathrm{mL}\) DMF (N,N-Dimethylformamide) for the uniform dispersion of GO in the solvent. Then \(50~\mathrm{mg}\) of GO was added into the above solution and sonicated for \(4\,\mathrm{{h}}\). Next, 1 mmol of \(\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}\cdot6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\), PTA (p-phthalic acid) and HF were dissolved in turn with stirring for \(20\;\mathrm{\min}\). The mixed solution was transferred into a Teflon-lined steel autoclave, which was placed in an oven at \(150\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(10\,\mathrm{h}\). Finally, the black columnar F-Co-MOF/rGO was washed with anhydrous methanol
for 3 times and dried in an oven at \(65\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) (Fig. S1).
2.2. Materials characterization.
XRD patterns were attained from a PANalytical X'Pert Pro within 2θ range from \(5^{\circ}\) to \(80^{\circ}\). The scanning electronic microscopy (SEM) images used to characterize the morphology and size of the target product were obtained using a field-emission scanning electron microscope (Zeiss Auriga 500). The TEM, HRTEM and mapping images were obtained by the transmission electron microscope (FEI TECNAIG2F20-S-TWIN) to further characterize the morphology, composition and element distribution of the samples. X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS, Thermo Scientific Escalab 250Xi) was used to test the type and chemical valance state of the elements on the surface of the sample, and all identified peaks were calibrated using the C1s standard energy spectrum at binding energy of \(284.8\;\mathrm{eV}\) on XPSpeak software. The thermal stability of the sample was tested by the thermos-gravimetric analysis (TGA, Simultaneous Thermal Analyzer, 409 PC) at temperature range of \(30{-}800\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) (Fig. S2).
2.3. Electrochemical characterization.
The hollow urchins F-Co-MOF/rGO were used as electrode materials for LIBs/SIBs by assembling a half cell in a glove box, and its electrochemical performance and lithium/sodium storage mechanism were tested. The active material (the weight is based on the F-Co-MOF/rGO) was mixed with acetylene black and PVDF (polyvinylidene fluoride) in a mass ratio of 80:10:10, and NMP (N-methyl-2-pyrrolidone) was used as a solvent to prepare a slurry by continuous grinding. The slurry was uniformly coated on a copper foil and dried at \(120\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for 8 hours under vacuum. It was cut into circular pieces
having a diameter of \(8\;\mathrm{mm}\) as a working electrode, and metallic lithium was used as a counter electrode to provide a sufficient amount of Li source. \(1\;\mathrm{mol}\;\mathrm{L}^{-1}\;\mathrm{LiPF}_{6}\) (or \(\mathrm{NaClO}_{4}\) for SIB) in ethylene carbonate/dimethyl carbonate/diethyl carbonate (EC: DMC: DEC) with a volume ratio of 1:1:1 as the electrolyte. When preparing the working electrode of sodium ion battery, in addition to the corresponding replacement of the counter electrode material lithium metal with sodium metal, the binder used is CMC (carboxymethylcellulose sodium). And similarly, the active material, acetylene black and CMC were mixed and ground in a mass ratio of 80:10:10 under the action of deionized water to make a slurry coated on the copper foil. The cycle and rate performance was evaluated using the LAND battery test system. The electrochemical workstation (CHI-600E) was used for cyclic voltammetry (CV) and electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) tests.
## 3. Results and discussion
The formation mechanism of F-Co-MOF/rGO with hollow urchin nanostructures is schematically illustrated in Fig. 1. First, the surfactant CTAB (hexadecyl trimethyl ammonium bromide) was added to the organic solvent DMF to improve the dispersion ability of GO in the system. The GO was added in the above solution and ultrasound continued until the solution became a clear and transparent homogeneous dispersion. After the addition of \(\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2} \cdot 6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\), due to the large number of oxygen-containing functional groups such as epoxy, hydroxyl and carboxyl groups on the surface of GO, the \(\mathrm{Co}^{2+}\) subjected to electrostatic adsorption will take the first place on the GO. This provides an appropriate site of activity for subsequent self-assembly reactions on rGO. Finally, the F-Co-MOF/rGO composite was obtained by adding the organic ligand PTA under the participation of F- through a high-temperature hydrothermal reaction process.
