The synthesized final product is characterized by PXRD to determine its physical composition. As presented in Fig. 2a, the F-Co-MOF and the F-Co-MOF/rGO composites demonstrate sharp and high intensity diffraction peaks at 2θ of \(8.9^{\circ}\), \(14.2^{\circ}\), \(15.9^{\circ}\) and \(17.9^{\circ}\), in which all the diffraction peaks of F-Co-MOF or F-Co-MOF/rGO can fit entirely with pristine Co-MOF (CCDC Number: 153067). It indicates that the desired product has been successfully synthesized and no other impurities are identified. So its molecular structure should be similar to \(\mathrm{Co}_{2}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}(\mathrm{C}_{8}\mathrm{O}_{4}\mathrm{H}_{4})\). In the XRD spectrum of the F-Co-MOF/rGO composite, no wide peak of rGO was found at \(24^{\circ}\) which may be caused by the low content of rGO or the characteristics of the composite itself. Fig. 2b displays Raman spectra of the pure rGO and the F-Co-MOF/rGO composite. Both samples manifest two peaks near \(1300~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) and \(1500~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\), which correspond to the D-band representing the degree of defect or disorder of GO and the G-band representing the
degree of order of graphite carbon, respectively [53]. Notably, the peak position of the F-Co-MOF/rGO composite would shift about \(10^{\circ}\) to the low wavenumber than that of GO (Fig. S3). This phenomenon indicates that the synergistic effect between F-Co-MOF and rGO enhances the charge transport capability between them [54]. Generally, the ratio \(\mathrm{I_{D}/I_{G}}\) of the strength of D-band and G-band is used to measure the disorder degree of carbon materials [55]. The \(\mathrm{I_{D}/I_{G}}\) value of pure rGO is 1.09 (GO is 0.96), while the value of the F-Co-MOF/rGO composite is 1.26, which indicates that the degree of defect and disorder is increased due to the successful reduction of GO [56].
The surface chemical compositions and the element valence states of F-Co-MOF/rGO were analyzed by XPS. According to the position of the peak in the XPS spectrum (Fig. 2c), the sample contains C, O, Co, F chemical elements. As displayed in Fig. 2d, two peaks of the Co 2p appear at 797.46 eV for Co \(2{\bf p}_{1/2}\) and \(781.33~\mathrm{eV}\) for Co \(2{\bf p}_{3/2}\), and at the same time they are accompanied by corresponding satellite peaks at 803.48 and 786.71 eV, respectively, which are in good agreement with the peaks of Co-MOF [57]. In Fig. 2e, there are four peaks located at 284.42, 285.03, 286.26, and 288.56 eV, which are indexed to the C-C, C-O, \(\mathrm{C}{=}\mathrm{O}\), \(\mathrm{O}{=}\mathrm{C}{-}\mathrm{O}\), respectively [58]. In addition, the high resolution O 1s XPS spectrum (Fig. 2f) of the F-Co-MOF/rGO composite can be fitted with two peaks, located at 531.6 and 533.2 eV, corresponding to the Co-O metal-oxygen bond and O-H hydroxyl functional group respectively [59]. It is also important to detect the presence of F element in XPS energy spectrum, which proves that the structure of the synthesized compound should be \(\mathrm{Co_{2}[F_{x}(O H)_{1-x}]_{2}(C_{8}O_{4}H_{4})}\).
The SEM images of the bare F-Co-MOF and the F-Co-MOF/rGO composite are demonstrated in Fig. 3a-d. The urchin nanostructures composed of short rods can be seen clearly through the observation under different magnification. This unique urchins nanostructure can provide a larger buffer space for volume expansion, which provides a strong support for its stable cycle performance and excellent rate capability. Due to the presence of rGO, the composite of F-Co-MOF/rGO displays slight agglomeration in Fig. 3c-d. TEM and HRTEM (Fig. 3e-g) were further characterized so as to observe the
structure and morphology of the composite material more intuitively. The hollow urchins F-Co-MOF are tightly surrounded by the transparent gauze-like rGO in Fig. 3e-f, which further confirm the formation of F-Co-MOF/rGO. It is worth noting that the highly shrunken rGO coated F-Co-MOF forms a three-dimensional continuous porous network structure, which may be beneficial to increase the conductivity of the hollow urchins F-Co-MOF during lithiation/delithiation and accommodate the volume expansion.
Fig. 3g displays the corresponding energy-dispersive X-ray spectrometry (EDS) elemental mappings of the F-Co-MOF/rGO samples corresponding to the STEM image, which demonstrates that the C (red), Co (green), F (yellow), and O (orange) elements evenly distributed and match well with the shape of the composites. In addition, the elemental mappings of the composite material also prove the successful synthesis of Co-MOF doped with F and coated with rGO and the network structure is formed by rGO. So these mapping data also confirm the previous XRD and XPS analysis results, showing that the molecular structure of the Co-MOF we synthesized is \(\mathrm{Co_{2}[F_{x}(OH)_{1-x}]_{2}(C_{8}O_{4}H_{4})}\). Elemental analysis test results show that the content of fluorine in F-Co-MOF is \(9.4\%\), and the content in F-Co-MOF/rGO is \(7.8\%\), so the unknown number X in the molecular formula should be close to 0.8.
