The lithium storage properties of F-Co-MOF and F-Co-MOF/rGO composite were tested between 0.01 and \(3.0\ \mathrm{V}\). Fig. 4a manifests the cyclic voltammetry (CV) profiles of the F-Co-MOF/rGO electrode at a scan rate of \(0.1\ \mathrm{mV\ s^{-1}}\). During the first scanning cycle, there are four cathode peaks appeared at 1.20, 1.07, 0.84 and 0.01 V. The very sharp peak at 0.01 V can be attributed to the insertion of \(\mathrm{Li^{+}}\) ions into the rGO. The broad oxidation peak at \(0.5\ \mathrm{V}\) corresponds to the extraction of \(\mathrm{Li^{+}}\) ions from the rGO. The reduction peak at \(1.3\ \mathrm{V}\) that has appeared and changed constantly after the first circle belongs to the reduction reaction of metal ions \((\mathrm{Co}^{2+}{\rightarrow}\mathrm{Co})\). The corresponding reverse reaction is the regeneration of metal ions, and the corresponding oxidation peak is at \(1.78\ \mathrm{V}\) [60-62]. Another major reduction peak at \(0.84\ \mathrm{V}\) is due to the carboxylate-to-enolate reaction.
Another major reduction peak at \(0.84\:\mathrm{V}\) is due to the carboxylate-to-enolate reaction. The reverse reaction of enolate-to-carboxylate corresponds to the oxidation peak at \(1.17\,\mathrm{V}\) [60, 63]. The wide reduction peaks at \(1.2\) and \(1.07\:\mathrm{V}\), which disappear after the first cycle, correspond to the formation of the solid electrolyte interface (SEI) film, which brings irreversible capacity loss. After the first cycle, the anode peaks at \(1.17\) and \(1.78\:\mathrm{V}\) would shift to the higher potentials, while the cathode peaks also move to the lower potential, which are caused by the polarization of the electrode material in the process of lithiation/delithiation. From the CV curves after the \(10^{\mathrm{th}}\) lap, it can be seen that the subsequent redox peaks have completely overlapped, showing the cycling stability of the F-Co-MOF/rGO electrode (Fig. S4). At the same time, the galvanostatic charge–discharge (GCD) of the F-Co-MOF/rGO electrode was tested and recorded at \(0.1\:\mathrm{A}\:\mathrm{g}^{-1}\). Notably, the redox peak measured by CV can correspond to the voltage plateau in the GCD curves. The CV and GCD of the pure F-Co-MOF electrode are presented in Fig. S5. The discharge capacity of the F-Co-MOF/rGO is \(2464.2\:\mathrm{mA}\:\mathrm{h}\:\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) with initial coulombic efficiency of \(73.45\%\) at \(0.1\:\mathrm{A}\:\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) (Fig. S5c). Compared to the F-Co-MOF, the coulombic efficiency of composite is increased by \(20\%\). The main causes of capacity loss are the irreversible decomposition of electrolytes and the formation of the SEI film on the surface of electrode. Under the same conditions, the rate capabilities of the F-Co-MOF and the F-Co-MOF/rGO electrodes at different current densities are tested and shown in Fig. 4c. When the current density is \(0.1\), \(0.2\), \(0.5\), \(1.0\), \(2\) and \(5\:\mathrm{A}\:\mathrm{g}^{-1}\), the F-Co-MOF electrode delivers an average discharge capacity of \(661.7\), \(598.6\), \(551.6\), \(374.9\) and \(253.6\:\mathrm{mA}\:\mathrm{h}\:\mathrm{g}^{-1}\), respectively. Most importantly, at the same current density gradient, the
capacity of the F-Co-MOF/rGO electrode is 1233.6, 1151.2, 994.5, 855.6, 726.2 and \(501.2\;\mathrm{mA}\;\mathrm{h}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\), respectively, increasing significantly. When the electrode current returns to \(0.1\;\mathrm{A}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) after undergoing the test of high current densities, the specific capacity can still reach \(1293.5\;\mathrm{mA}\;\mathrm{h}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\), demonstrating its excellent rate performance. The effective increase of capacity may be due to the synergistic effect of rGO and F-Co-MOF. In addition, the \(\mathrm{Li}^{+}\)ions insertion/desertion mechanisms of rGO and F-Co-MOF just promote each other. Fig. 4e shows the Nyquist EIS patterns of the F-Co-MOF and the F-Co-MOF/rGO electrodes. The EIS is composed of the semicircles at high frequency zone and a straight line at low frequency zone. And the diameter of semicircle in the high frequency reflects the charge transfer resistance, while the slope of the straight line in the low frequency corresponds to the diffusion state of \(\mathrm{Li}^{+}\)ions [64]. Obviously, the F-Co-MOF/rGO electrode has a smaller resistance, from which it also inferred that the F-Co-MOF/rGO electrode will have better electrochemical performance. Owing to the fast kinetics, the F-Co-MOF/rGO electrode would give impressive long-term cycle stability. At a current density of \(2\;\mathrm{A}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\), the F-Co-MOF/rGO electrode nearly suffers no capacity degradation over 550 cycles with a high capacity retention of \(97.6\%\) compared to the value of the second cycle (Fig. 4d), which is \(196.5\%\) higher than the reversible capacity of \(392.6\;\mathrm{mA}\;\mathrm{h}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) of the F-Co-MOF electrode. Pure rGO also works for 550 cycles at a current density of \(2\;\mathrm{A}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) (Fig. S7), and the reversible capacity is only \(152.2\;\mathrm{mA}\;\mathrm{h}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\), which indicates that the unusual performance of composite materials mainly comes from F-Co-MOF. In addition, elemental analysis test results show that the rGO content in the F-Co-MOF/rGO is \(24\%\), which also proves that the capacity of rGO will not be the main
capacity contributor of the F-Co-MOF/rGO. The coulombic efficiency of the F-Co-MOF/rGO electrode exceeds \(99\%\) after 5 discharge-charge cycles, and the coulombic efficiency of the F-Co-MOF electrode does not reach \(99\%\) until 30 cycles, which also reflects the former has a better reversible lithium storage capacity among MOFs electrode materials reported up to date (Table S1). Co-MOF without F was also synthesized and tested for its electrochemical performance (Fig. S6). The test results show that the electrochemical performance of F-Co-MOF is greatly improved (compared to Co-MOF), and proves that this F-doped MOF strategy to improve electrochemical performance is very effective.
