of the pseudocapacitive shown by the purple region at a scan rate of \(0.8\;\mathrm{mV}\;\mathrm{s}^{-1}\).
To gain further insight into its kinetic properties, CV curves of F-Co-MOF/rGO electrode were also recorded at various sweep rates from 0.2 to \(1.0\;\mathrm{mV}\;\mathrm{s}^{-1}\) (Fig. 5a). It can be seen from the CV curves that with the increase of scanning rate, the anode peak shifts to a higher potential and the cathode peak shifts to a lower potential, which is concerned with the impacts of polarization due to the restrained kinetics of lithium diffusion across the anode materials. In the CV curves, according to the current at the main oxidation peak and reduction peak and their corresponding scanning rate, the linear relationship between Log \((i)\) and Log \((\nu)\) shown in Fig. 5b is obtained on the basis of the following equations 1 and 2 [1, 65].
\[
i=av^{b}
\]
\[
\mathrm{Log}(i)=b\mathrm{Log}(v)+\mathrm{Log}(a)
\]
Where \(i\) is the peak current, \(v\) is potential sweep rate, \(a\) and \(b\) are adjustable parameters. When the value of \(b\) is close to 1, the electrochemical reaction is dominated by pseudocapacitance. The \(b\)-values are calculated to be 0.867 and 0.883 for the cathodic and anodic peak, respectively (Fig. 5b), suggesting of a dominant pseudocapacitive contribution during reversible charge and discharge processes. This will facilitate its fast \(\mathrm{Li}^{+}\)intercalation/extraction and long cycle stability, under a high current density. The contribution percentage of the pseudocapacitive charge storage can be calculated by below equation
\[
i(V)=k_{1}v+k_{2}v^{1/2}
\]
Where \(\mathrm{i(V)}\) is the measured current at a fixed potential \(\mathrm{V}\); \(v\) indicates the scan rate; and both \(k_{1}\) and \(k_{2}\) are adjustable parameters. From the above formula, it can be seen that
the current \( i \) at the scan rate \( v \) can be divided into two parts, \( k_{1}v \) represents the contribution of capacitance, and \( k_{2}v^{1/2} \) represents the contribution of diffusion control. Fig. 5c shows the calculated pseudocapacitive contribution at different scan rates, the contribution percentages are \( 55\% \), \( 59\% \), \( 62\% \), \( 65\% \), and \( 69\% \) at the scan rates of 0.2, 0.4, 0.6, 0.8, and \( 1.0\ \mathrm{mV\ s^{-1}} \), respectively. Fig. 5d particularly shows the contribution of pseudocapacitance at a scan rate of \( 0.8\ \mathrm{mV\ s^{-1}} \), the black line represents the total current obtained through the experimental test, and the purple area represents the current controlled by the pseudocapacitive behavior obtained through calculation. According to the test results and calculation analysis, the lithium storage of the F-Co-MOF/rGO electrode is mainly contributed by surface-controlled rather than diffusion-controlled, which is consistent with its excellent rate performance.
