of F-Co-MOF/rGO electrode; (d) Cycling stability of F-Co-MOF/rGO electrode at \(0.1\,\mathrm{~A~g~}^{-1}\); (e) Nyquist plots of F-Co-MOF/rGO electrode.
Sodium-ion batteries (SIBs), which are expected to replace LIBs in future energy storage applications, are important energy storage devices. Because of its low cost, abundant resources and lower redox potential of \(\mathrm{Na^{+}/Na}\) (-2.7 V), the SIB has become a research hotspot in recent years [25, 66-70]. Considering that F-Co-MOF/rGO manifests impressive electrochemical performance in LIBs, its performance in SIBs is also worth investigating. Fig. 6a displays the CV curves of the first four cycles at a scan rate of \(0.1\,\mathrm{mV\,s^{-1}}\). During the first scanning cycle, there are four reduction peaks. Among them, except for the \(0.77\,\mathrm{V}\) that disappeared after the first cycle is attributed to the formation of the SEI film, the other three peaks at \(1.08\), \(0.53\), and \(0.01\,\mathrm{V}\) can be assigned to insertion of \(\mathrm{Na^{+}}\) ions. The three oxidation peaks at \(0.08\), \(0.64\) and \(1.15\,\mathrm{V}\) correspond to the extraction of \(\mathrm{Na^{+}}\) ions. Fig. 6b presents the GCD profiles for different cycles at \(0.1\,\mathrm{A\,g^{-1}}\), compared to lithium storage, it can be seen that the F-Co-MOF/rGO has a lower discharge platform in the first cycle during storing sodium. This is because \(\mathrm{Na^{+}/Na}\) have a higher redox potential than \(\mathrm{Li^{+}/Li}\) (-3.014 V). It can be seen from EIS spectrum in Fig. 6c that the resistance of F-CO-MOF/rGO electrode in Na-ion half cells is greater than that in Li-ion half cells, which may be because \(\mathrm{Na^{+}}\) (0.102 nm) has a larger atomic radius than \(\mathrm{Li^{+}}\) (0.076 nm), leading to greater resistance in the cycling process [64]. This also explains why the rate capability of F-Co-MOF/rGO electrode in Na-ion half cells is not as good as that in Li-ion half cells. As displayed in the Fig. 6c, when the current density is \(0.1\), \(0.2\), \(0.5\), \(1.0\) and \(2.0\,\mathrm{A\,g^{-1}}\), the F-Co-MOF/rGO electrode delivers an average discharge
capacity of 354.5, 264.3, 218.9, 174.8 and \(128.9\;\mathrm{mA}\) h \(\mathrm{g}^{-1}\), respectively. Considering the current situation that the sodium storage performance exhibited by F-Co-MOF/rGO is still relatively good among MOFs (Table S1). Fig. 6d presents a cyclic performance at a small current density of \(0.1\;\mathrm{A}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\), which can retain a reversible capacity of \(181.6\;\mathrm{mA}\) h \(\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) after 100 cycles with a high coulombic efficiency of \(99.98\%\). Although F-Co-MOF/rGO material has a higher discharge capacity at the beginning, which is \(428.6\;\mathrm{mA}\) h \(\mathrm{g}^{-1}\), after 100 cycles of continuous attenuation, the capacity retention rate is \(50\%\). Although the electrochemical performance of F-Co-MOF/rGO in SIBs is not as superior as that in LIBs, this active exploration of MOFs in SIBs is overwhelmingly valuable.
performance at \(0.2\;\mathrm{A}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\).
In view of the excellent electrochemical performance of F-Co-MOF/rGO in Li-ion half cells, it is a strong support for conducting full cell tests. Lithium iron phosphate (LiFePO\(_4\)) has the advantages of high theoretical specific capacity, very safety, and stable performance, so the F-Co-MOF/rGO composite used as anode material and LiFePO\(_4\) as cathode for full cell. Fig. 7a shows the discharge-charge voltage profile of LiFePO\(_4\) as the working electrode (lithium metal is used as the counter electrode) of a half cell in the voltage range of \(2.0{-}4.0\;\mathrm{V}\) with a current density of \(0.2\;\mathrm{A}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\). The electrochemical performance of the as prepared full cell are manifested in Fig. 7b-c. As shown in Fig. 7c, the full cell still can retain a reversible capacity of \(165.2\;\mathrm{mA}\;\mathrm{h}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) after 300 cycles at the current density of \(0.2\;\mathrm{A}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\). It is worth noting that the capacity attenuation of the last 200 cycles is not as obvious as that of the first 100 cycles, which demonstrates the potential of the F-Co-MOF/rGO electrode in LIBs.
## 4. Conclusion
In summary, we have synthesized F doping Co-MOF composite materials with hollow urchin nanostructures by a simple solvothermal method, and obtained excellent electrochemical performance in both half and full cells. Importantly, the F-Co-MOF/rGO electrode in half cells can deliver a high specific capacity of \(1202.0\;\mathrm{mA}\;\mathrm{h}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) at \(0.1\;\mathrm{A}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) with a coulombic efficiency of \(99.95\%\) after 50 cycles. Even at a current density of \(2\;\mathrm{A}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\), a high reversible capacity of \(771.5\;\mathrm{mA}\;\mathrm{h}\;\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) can still be achieved. In view of the excellent electrochemical performance of F-Co-MOF/rGO in half cells, we chose a cathode material LiFePO\(_4\) with stable performance to match it, and assembled it into a
full cell testing. The full cell gives the excellent cycle performance with a reversible capacity of \(165.2\,\mathrm{mA\,h\,g^{-1}}\) after 300 cycles under a current density of \(0.2\,\mathrm{A\,g^{-1}}\). In addition, the F-Co-MOF/rGO also manifested a certain sodium storage capacity. The reversible capacity was maintained at \(181.2\,\mathrm{mA\,h\,g^{-1}}\) after 100 cycles under a current density of \(0.1\,\mathrm{A\,g^{-1}}\), which is of great significance for exploring the application of MOFs in SIBs. The F-Co-MOF/rGO electrode showed impressive electrochemical performance in both LIBs and SIBs, which was attributed to two aspects. On the one hand, it benefits from the synergistic effect of F-Co-MOF with high conductive rGO networks. On the other hand, F doping can improve the reversibility of lithiation/delithiation and lithium storage performance. This simple and effective strategy can provide reference for the synthesis of other MOFs with high performance.
